lling merchants rather than markets: \'The system of tsarist administration was no different here from what existed in any other part of Northern Siberia. The Dolgans, Yakuts and Evenks had to pay the fur-tax as “natives” and formed “clans” headed by princelings, while the tundra peasants were forced to pay a poll tax, were formed into a “community” and headed by an elder. People living many hundreds of kilometers away from the nearest centers were economically dependent on the merchants who monopolized supplies to the region, bought up all the furs, and cruelly exploited the population.\'  This aspect combined with Sakha transhumanism suggests an absence of permanent markets.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 971, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 881})
